**Amendment (Actor conflict recovery)** · `amend:harness:actor-retry-proposal` An act attempt spends the public author/sequence slot of (`amend:identity:act-stops`) only by entering the authoritative order. The Actor keeps its current original proposal, N, returned realization and approval in protected working custody, not a durable intention journal. The host may transiently process several bounded attempts at an unspent slot, including different families or content; each independently satisfies (`alg:design:authentication-handoff`) before sealing. A seal promises no inclusion. With its working state intact the Actor approves at most one exact realization per slot and resends retained bytes rather than silently approving another. If state is lost it may approve a fresh attempt at the same slot; ordering still admits at most one, and either approved realization may win. Choosing one is not a host fault or evidence of substitution under network-v0.31.1, premise{``def:graph:approval-witness``; approval binds one realization but promises no final-set membership}. The winner must be exactly one approved complete act, including both hyper-edge projections, under premise{``itm:graph:act-authentication-requirements-proposal-integrity``; substitution of the approved proposal is detectable} and premise{``itm:graph:act-authentication-requirements-unity``; both projections share one approved parent act}. Fresh attempts do not retract earlier approvals or guarantee replacement; cancellation of off-record work is not revocation of bytes another party already holds. After ordering, even an identical submission receives SpentActSlot with the winning public act and placement; the client reads that act rather than causing another debit. Before ordering, a complete prefix showing no winner is only a snapshot, never a guarantee that an earlier approved attempt cannot subsequently win. An unavailable or unauthenticated prefix is Unknown/Unavailable, never evidence that a slot is free. Do not advance to a different slot as a retry of an unresolved attempt. This is at-most-once per slot, not exactly-once execution of a human intention, recovery of lost content, or uniqueness of equal content submitted at different slots. Unlike (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`), which must retain spent-output intersections because later attempts can choose different inputs, every act attempt carries its fixed conflict key in public structural identity; no note is needed to preserve that intersection. The capsule, its reservation, pending-request enumeration, sealing key and host custody of sealed Actor state are withdrawn; no local-file journal replaces them. No attempt that fails to become an act leaves a durable request, realization or outcome record. Its transient private material is released under the custody rules. The host's counter gaps and the ordinary removable private projections of an ordered act are not records of pending intentions. Handshake transport remains the same CBOR-native GraphQL execution boundary with a separately authenticated private custody context, not public GraphQL variables or traced secret content; it admits no parallel storage or RPC adapter. Losing the original proposal prevents approval of an old returned seal: start a fresh complete attempt, never guess equality. Production signing, entropy, private transport, ordered persistence and host allocation remain held until their own evidence and reviews.

| Loss or concurrency point | Client action | Public result and limit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| State is lost before proposal. | Re-read complete history, select the unspent slot and construct a fresh proposal with fresh N. Lost content must be supplied again; it is not recovered. | No act exists unless some other approved attempt wins that slot. |
| Proposal or its reply is lost before a seal is received. | With working state intact, resend or restart at the same slot; without it, construct a fresh attempt. | A new realization may use new host salts. A proposal or seal alone cannot be ordered. |
| State or the seal reply is lost before approval. | Do not approve the old realization without the original body and openings. Start a fresh attempt at the same slot. | Unapproved realizations stay off record; no host retention promise is needed. |
| Approval, submission or the ordering reply is lost. | Look for the winner in authenticated order. If there is none in the current complete prefix, retry the same slot; an earlier approved attempt may still win. | SpentActSlot names the actual winner. Absence at a prefix is not cancellation, nor permission to retry the same uncertain action at a different slot. |
| A public lookup reply is lost or incomplete. | Repeat the public lookup or keep the same conflicting slot; never interpret missing evidence as a spent or globally free slot. | The authoritative record decides the winner, not a response log. |
| Working state and any optional client seed are lost, but an authorized signing key remains. | Select the public run and author, recover the spent-slot set and use fresh OS entropy. | The selected scheme requires no derivation seed. The key alone may not identify which shared-key author the user intended. |
| A losing attempt carries a newer retirement point. | Read the committed winner; name that point again in a later ordinary act at an unused slot. | The losing attempt was not ordered, so its point has no effect. |
| Two devices act for one author. | They may choose the same unused slot and each approve a complete attempt. They resolve the winner from order before treating the slot as spent. | Atomic ordering admits at most one. Approvals at different slots can both be ordered; there is no cross-slot deduplication of human intent. |

**Amendment (Wallet spending capability)** · `amend:harness:wallet-spending-capability` The daemon study established that comments do not enforce request uniqueness, raw signed drafts need not enter wallet history, and method whitelists do not scope a loaded wallet. Those observations remain valid, but uniqueness no longer depends on a daemon request-association facility. Under (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`), consensus over a nonempty common input set makes attempts mutually exclusive; the only retained attempt state is its owned spent outputs, never a log asserting payment outcomes. The signer is a dedicated descriptor wallet using PSET, with chain reads and explicit broadcast; best-effort disjoint retries and outcome journals remain forbidden. The real-daemon conflict witness in (`verif:harness:descriptor-recovery`) establishes its finite test histories, not a production wallet implementation or crash-atomic note store. Bite eight's production spending cut still owes durable all-writer admission, cancellation and crash ordering, complete descriptor discovery and bounded scans. The former requirement for atomically unique wallet comments and complete wallet-history lookup is withdrawn because neither is the uniqueness authority.

**Verification (Wallet capability study)** · `verif:harness:wallet-capability-study` The real regtest study distinguishes primitive facilities from the complete spending contract in (`amend:harness:wallet-spending-capability`). It checks method-whitelist intersection and denied methods, credential rotation, cross-wallet method authority, raw-draft lookup absence, exact wallet-created bytes across restart, abandoned lookup, bounded pagination and concurrent repeated-request acceptance. The fixture funds independent confirmed inputs before disabling broadcast; the study never admits a production spending method. Its public observation artifact records the resulting transaction locators and capability outcomes without credentials. The obligation is limited to those finite controls and declares no witnessed paper labels. The service-case allowance in (`tab:devperf:budgets`) measures this public case through a discarded warm-up and serial warm samples under (`conv:design:case-budget-calibration`), including its daemon lifecycle. The allowance changes no assertion, spending boundary or tier budget.

**Verification (Descriptor recovery)** · `verif:harness:descriptor-recovery` The finite real-daemon history checks (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`) with a dedicated descriptor wallet signing PSETs. Different amounts, fees and input sets retain a common input; an attempt sharing only with its predecessor refuses before the signer is called. A lost broadcast reply cannot produce another confirmation, a confirmed return-to-wallet spend invalidates earlier signatures, and concurrent writers proposing the same unused inputs admit exactly one request before signing. A barrier releases both proposals together; the losing writer neither signs nor acquires a reservation. The separate disjoint pair still signs, broadcasts and confirms both requests. A shallow reorg changes which request attempt confirms without making both count. Recovery discards request notes and signed attempts, then scans the known descriptor scripts against the complete fixture chain and pending pool without wallet history. The witness proves this finite history, not arbitrary descriptor expansion or crash durability of its test note file. Its obligation declares no witnessed paper labels. The service-case allowance in (`tab:devperf:budgets`) measures the public case through the warm campaign in (`conv:design:case-budget-calibration`), including its complete daemon lifecycle. It remains in the real-service tier under (`rule:harness:tiers`); neither its assertions nor any tier budget changes.

**Amendment (Authentication recovery review needs)** · `amend:harness:authentication-review-needs` Review (`amend:harness:actor-retry-proposal`) against the following obligations rather than an encrypted recovery protocol. The public generator requires independently selected run and author identity and complete authenticated ordered history; a signing key is not an account locator because delegates can be shared or rotated. Public token-history discovery may identify candidate authors, but the client must choose the intended identity; no secret registry is supplied. Public slots are regenerated, private attempts are not. Fresh OS entropy for N remains the selected rule of (`amend:identity:act-concealment`). Network-v0.31.1, premise{``case:deployment:concealment-entropy-secret-derived``; secret derivation is permitted under a published entropy rule}, permits an alternative, not an already admitted recipe. Deterministic derivation would require a high-entropy independent seed, a separately admitted domain-separated keyed construction over the complete canonical attempt, and protection against repetition across different bodies, private projections, runs and editions. Equal content would reproduce the nonce only while that seed and every input survive; a seed disclosed later would permit candidate-content derivation and joint search against P, defeating the intended removal concealment for enumerable inputs. A signing key is not that seed. This design admits no derivation seed or operation: source failure refuses a new attempt, and loss of any optional external seed does not affect this fresh-entropy route. A future admitted seed would belong in an Actor protected home under (`dec:crypto:authentication-custody`), with its keyed-operation exit separately reviewed, not in the host or ordinary client files. The existing format checks cannot prove unpredictability; production source provenance and failure evidence remain required under (`req:crypto:authentication-custody-evidence`). Independent controls must establish two pending approvals but one ordered winner, cross-family conflicts, concurrent writers, lost replies at every stage, complete-prefix lookup, exact typed duplicate witnesses, rollback/CommitUnknown and no double debit. A duplicate-slot witness establishes a public uniqueness fault, not the paper's dependency-opening fraud proof. No claim is made about attempts absent from the record. Public verification and pure recovery fixtures precede 8-e; live atomic ordering and the host salt allocator require its reviewed durability, with separate private-state admission. The three signed message recipes and the retirement rule are unchanged. The public slot-generation cut has two held entry inputs owned by its client/publication design: an operation or client traversal that supplies one author’s complete ordered history with completeness evidence, and the parameter bounding that traversal and its arithmetic. Existing chain-scan limits do not supply an act-history bound. Neither source nor parameter is admitted by this design. realizes{``amend:identity:act-concealment``; The review obligations retain the fresh-entropy requirement without claiming that the requirement establishes unpredictability.}

| Recovery obligation | Decision in the conflict design |
| --- | --- |
| Public intent key and idempotent reservation | The public slot is the conflict key. There is no intent reservation; only committed order spends it. |
| Host realization retention and identical-retry answer | No durable pending realization is retained. A fresh handshake may differ; an ordered retry returns SpentActSlot and the winning public act, even for identical bytes. |
| One act per author sequence | The final all-writer ordering transaction enforces this across families and epochs; proposal checks do not reserve it. |
| Durable compare-and-set stage transitions | No pending-stage store exists. Approval requires the current original proposal; loss starts a fresh attempt. |
| Uncertain-commit resolution | Only authoritative order and the host allocator have durable effects. Their existing serialization and CommitUnknown contracts must resolve before contradictory order or allocation can escape. |
| Unrecoverable-sequence tombstone | No capsule or tombstone is needed. An unspent slot remains usable; a spent slot is evidenced by its ordered act. |
| Capsule access credential and recipe | There is no capsule access or enumeration operation and no new credential or signed recipe. Ordinary private handshake authentication remains held. |
| Sealing, key custody, associated data, nonce uniqueness, padding and coupled removal | Capsule cryptography is absent. Fresh proposal entropy, ordinary handshake custody, host salt allocation and removal of ordered private projections retain their separate obligations. |

**Amendment (Author token conformance)** · `amend:harness:author-token-conformance` Extend the future authentication cases, not the already executed burn claims, with the contract of (`rule:devnet:author-token`) and (`rule:identity:author-key-history`). Independent fixtures must cover issuance qualification, amount-one/no-reissuance, display and consensus byte order, explicit continuations, omitted spends, malformed reserved declarations contributing no key without disabling other declarations or co-moving tokens, empty and repeated declarations, shared transactions followed by diverging histories, inclusive retirement and repeated keys across its boundary, loss/disclosure recovery through a later delegate, and an empty active set. Verify all three signed objects against two distinct authors sharing one key. Check fixed-prefix repeatability, epoch k authorization followed by k+1 retirement, the maximum path position among same-author acts with differing points, first-act issuance default, every family carrying the field, unchanged identity and target handling, removal independence, ordinary authenticated acts with unknown/backward points retained with no effect, no later activation of an absent reference, unchanged earlier acts, pending forks and a finality-premise breach. Tests establish deterministic conformance, robustness, fault tolerance and test quality; they cannot establish availability or chain finality. The same semantic cases later run through 8-d2 and descriptor-backed 8-h. Recovery and durable retirement placement require 8-e. The recovery controls of (`amend:harness:authentication-review-needs`) must revalidate any candidate against its admitting epoch's authority view after a retirement or prefix change. A fresh attempt may replace lost working state, but an old signed proposal is never edited; it may still win if eligible and the slot remains unspent.
